From a121797bf2001f567489d4ec238ed30610bfdd8c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: forkmesh <forkmesh@users.forkmesh.local>
Date: Sat, 18 Jul 2026 13:32:14 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 03/80] Radar: draw mirror-node blips as crisp, evenly-spaced
 shapes

The relay-radar blips were hard to read: each node was placed at a
hash-derived angle (so blips clustered/overlapped) and was only drawn
while the sweep had recently crossed it, as a soft translucent halo that
faded to nothing. At any instant most of the roster was invisible and
what showed was fuzzy.

Now every mirror node is always drawn as a solid, outlined dot (or amber
triangle when failing the integrity gate), spaced evenly around a single
ring by its index so the whole roster is separable at a glance. The sweep
keeps a brief brighten as it passes, layered behind the crisp shape.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 qt_client/src/MainWindowInternal.h | 75 ++++++++++++++++--------------
 1 file changed, 40 insertions(+), 35 deletions(-)

diff --git a/qt_client/src/MainWindowInternal.h b/qt_client/src/MainWindowInternal.h
index 6f4e57e4..23306404 100644
--- a/qt_client/src/MainWindowInternal.h
+++ b/qt_client/src/MainWindowInternal.h
@@ -1339,58 +1339,63 @@ protected:
         p.setBrush(accent);
         p.drawEllipse(c, 1.4, 1.4);
 
