+            "body": "## Outcome\n\nImport, continuously mirror, and export a hosted repository through one provider-neutral adapter contract while preserving context and reporting every lossy conversion.\n\n## Implementation\n\n- Define adapter capabilities for Git refs, issues, pull requests, reviews, comments, labels, milestones, discussions, documentation snapshots, workflow definitions and checks, releases and assets, and portable social records.\n- Implement one-action onboarding from a standard Git URL plus an adapter credential, with resumable cursors and idempotent retries.\n- Support two-way ref and collaboration synchronization with origin markers, conflict records, and loop prevention.\n- Preserve source identifiers, timestamps, relationships, attachments, and authorship status without impersonating source signatures; sign local importer provenance.\n- Produce a signed migration report listing imported, updated, skipped, unsupported, unverifiable, and failed records plus a resilience summary linked to #439 and #466.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- A repeated import with the same cursor creates no duplicate refs or records.\n- Concurrent changes on both sides produce an explicit conflict instead of silent last-write-wins loss.\n- Interrupted code, metadata, and asset transfers resume from durable cursors.\n- Unsupported source fields are preserved in the report and never presented as migrated.\n- The adapter core has no source-provider names or provider-specific branches; adapter fixtures supply all variance.\n\n## Dependencies\n\nUses #440 for deterministic records, #461 for releases, and #464/#471 for event and check exchange.\n\n## Verification\n\nRound-trip a fixture repository containing every supported record type through an in-process adapter, interrupt each phase, replay it, and compare refs, relationships, provenance, assets, cursors, and the signed report.",
