+            "title": "Publish deployment boundaries and independent service bootstrap",
+            "body": "## Outcome\n\nMake every service boundary and failure mode machine-readable, then prove the network can run without the repository's default public deployment.\n\n## Implementation\n\n- Define a deployment manifest for canonical, cached, transient, encrypted, and observable data plus retention and operator ownership.\n- Package independently configurable relay and control, public index and search, web, seed, identity resolution, room relay, workflow broker, and organization service roles.\n- Provide one declarative bootstrap that can colocate roles or point each role at an independent operator.\n- Add signed configuration export and import, health, backup boundaries, and conformance checks for every role.\n- Document and test identity resolution, public discovery, cloning, contribution routing, and recovery when the default public services disappear.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- A clean self-host fixture starts every role without a hidden dependency on a default public endpoint.\n- Removing the default control plane leaves existing repositories cloneable and independently discoverable.\n- The manifest states exactly which metadata each role can and cannot observe.\n- Canonical Git and collaboration data remain with configured repository nodes.\n- Configuration migration rejects secret leakage, unknown roles, and incompatible schema versions.\n\n## Dependencies\n\nBuilds on #442, #456, #473, and protocol schemas from #474.\n\n## Verification\n\nStart a full independent topology, import configuration, clone and contribute, remove each role and the default public endpoints in turn, restore it, and verify declared failure behavior and data boundaries.",
