+            "id": "open-474",
+            "title": "Publish executable protocol specifications and a threat model",
+            "body": "## Outcome\n\nMake the repository protocol independently implementable with versioned schemas, canonical vectors, a threat model, and a small reference verifier.\n\n## Implementation\n\n- Publish normative specifications for identities and rotation, repository identity and manifests, collaboration and social events, receipts, private envelopes, relay and peer streams, migration, releases, and workflow checks.\n- Generate JSON Schemas plus HTTP and WebSocket interface descriptions from one versioned source.\n- Document trust boundaries, assets, adversaries, metadata exposure, replay, rollback, equivocation, denial-of-service, key loss, and recovery assumptions.\n- Provide positive and negative canonical signing, encryption, fold, transport, and migration vectors.\n- Implement a dependency-light reference verifier that validates schemas, signatures, hashes, event folds, manifests, receipts, and stream frames without using production client code.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- Every sprint object and endpoint has a version, compatibility rule, size bound, and unknown-version behavior.\n- Production implementations and the reference verifier agree on every positive vector and reject every negative vector.\n- Generated schemas and interface descriptions cannot drift from the normative source.\n- The threat model states residual risks and never claims encryption can revoke already decrypted data.\n- A clean independent implementation can verify a repository snapshot using only the published artifacts.\n\n## Dependencies\n\nDeveloped alongside all contract issues; #443 makes the complete specification and verifier a release blocker.\n\n## Verification\n\nGenerate all artifacts twice, compare normalized bytes, run every vector in each production implementation and the reference verifier, mutate every signed field, and verify documented version and size failures.",
